ga8D(){return"Percentage of the day elapsed in the selected timezone. Resets to 0 at midnight."},
ga8E(){return"Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone. Resets to 0 at midnight."},
ga8F(){return"Decimal time, in use for a few years at the end of the 18th century in France, divides the day into 10 decimal hours. An hour has 100 decimal minutes, a minute 100 decimal seconds."},
ga8G(){return"The Doomsday Clock is a symbol representing how close humanity is to self-destruction. Maintained by the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists. Latest setting: 85 seconds to midnight (January 2026). With Time Shift, historical settings are shown."},
ga8H(){return"Dublin Julian Date (DJD) = JD \u2212 2,415,020 (epoch: 1899-12-31 12:00 UT). Defined by the International Astronomical Union (IAU) in 1955 at its Dublin meeting as a convenient day count for astronomical calculations."},
ga8I(){return"Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (GMST) \u2013 the hour angle of the vernal equinox at Greenwich. Used by astronomers to determine which part of the sky is overhead."},
ga8J(){return"GPS time counts seconds since 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC and does not include leap seconds. It is 18 seconds ahead of UTC."},
ga8K(){return"Seconds since midnight, shown in base 16 (hexadecimal), grouped in pairs for readability."},
ga8L(){return"Julian Date (JD) counts days since noon on January 1, 4713 BC (epoch: -4712-01-01 12:00:00 UT). The standard time reference in astronomy for calculating intervals between events."},
ga8M(){return"Local Mean Sidereal Time (LMST) \u2013 the hour angle of the vernal equinox at your location. Derived from GMST by adding your longitude. Used by astronomers to determine which objects are currently observable."},
ga8N(){return"Modified Julian Date (MJD) = JD \u2212 2,400,000.5 (epoch: 1858-11-17 00:00:00 UT). Starts at midnight rather than noon, and has smaller numbers. Introduced by the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory in 1957 for satellite tracking, including the orbit of Sputnik."},
ga8O(){return"Modified Julian Date 2000 (MJD2000) = JD \u2212 2,451,544.5 or MJD \u2212 51,544 (epoch: 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC). Used in geodesy, geophysics, and spaceflight to simplify calculations with smaller numbers by shifting the reference to the beginning of the year 2000."},
ga8P(){return"New Earth Time (or NET), also called degree time, splits a day into 360 NET degrees, where each NET degree has 60 NET minutes and each NET minute 60 NET seconds. UTC is the only time zone."},
ga8Q(){return"Seconds since midnight, shown in base 8 (octal)."},
ga8R(){return"Reduced Julian Date (RJD) = JD \u2212 2,400,000 (epoch: 1858-11-16 12:00 UT). A simple reduction of the Julian Date used mainly in astronomy to make the numbers shorter, while retaining the original noon-based start of the Julian day."},
ga8S(){return"The current time in the selected timezone, shown in Roman numerals. N, the initial of nulla or nihil (Latin for 'none' or 'nothing'), is used to represent zeros."},
ga8T(){return"The current time in the selected timezone in alarm clock design."},
ga8U(){return"Swatch Internet Time divides the day into 1000 .beats. There are no time zones \u2014 the whole world shares the same .beat. Based on UTC+1 (Biel Mean Time)."},
ga8V(){return"International Atomic Time (Temps Atomique International in French, TAI) is a continuous atomic time scale without leap seconds, maintained by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (Bureau International des Poids et Mesures, BIPM). For display as seconds, the app uses the Unix epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC) as its numerical reference. TAI is 37 seconds ahead of UTC. The last, and final, leap second was added on 2016-12-31 at 23:59:60 UTC."},
ga8W(){return"The current time in the selected timezone."},
ga8X(){return"Seconds elapsed since the Unix epoch: 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC."},
